WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.279
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:07.710
This is going to be a weird, non-traditional
episode.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.669
Not the usual Two Minute Papers.

00:00:09.669 --> 00:00:13.619
Hope you’ll enjoy it and if you finished
the video, please let me know in the comments

00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:15.179
what you think about it.

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:16.290
So let's start.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.290
Life lessons I learned from ai research.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:22.140
Number one, you need an objective.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.670
Before we start training a neural network
to perform, for instance, image classification,

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.250
we need a bunch of training data.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:35.280
This we can feed to this neural network, telling
that this image depicts a cat, and this one

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.530
is not a cat, but an ostrich.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.449
We also need to specify a loss function.

00:00:40.449 --> 00:00:45.899
This is super important, because this loss
function is used to make sure that the neural

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:51.410
network trains itself in a way that its predictions
will be similar to the training data it is

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.489
being fed.

00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:58.109
It is also referred to as an objective or
objective function to indicate that we know

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:02.920
precisely what we are looking for and that’s
what the neural network should do.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.880
This is a way to measure how the neural network
is progressing, and without this, it is useless.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.580
Similarly, in an other learning problem, we
specify an objective for this ant, which,

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:21.080
in this case, is to be able to traverse as
far from a starting as possible, and it reconfigures

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.840
its body type and movement to be able to score
high on our objective.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:32.140
And, this leads us to the second lesson: a
change in the objective changes the strategy

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.300
required to achieve it.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:39.610
Look here - in a different problem definition,
we can specify a different objective, for

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:44.690
instance, a different terrain, and you see
that if we wish to succeed here, we need a

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.140
vastly different body type.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.560
Form follows function.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.820
And in this other case, the objective is to
be able to traverse efficiently, but with

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.650
minimal material use for the legs.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:01.830
The solution, again, changes accordingly to
the objective.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:05.300
New objectives require new strategies.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:10.350
Number three: If the objective was wrong,
do not worry, and aim again!

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:11.800
Have a look at AlphaGo.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:16.390
This is DeepMind’s algorithm that was able
to defeat some of the best players in the

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:18.580
world in the game of Go.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:24.300
This was a highly non-trivial achievement
as the space of possible moves is so stupendously

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:27.910
large that it is impossible to evaluate every
move.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:33.350
Instead, it tries to aggressively focus on
a smaller number of possible moves, and tries

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.910
to simulate the result of these moves.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:41.130
If the move leads to an improvement in our
position, it is a good one, if not, it should

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.310
be avoided.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:44.420
Sounds simple, right?

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:50.780
Well, we have an objective, that’s great,
but, initially, it has a really bad predictor,

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:56.040
which means that it is really bad at judging
which move is good and which one isn’t.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.750
However, over time, it refines its predictor
and these estimations improve further and

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:02.790
further.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.560
In the end, by only taking a brief look at
the state of the game, it can predict with

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.050
a high confidence whether it is going to win
or not.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:17.240
Initially, we have an objective, but how do
we know whether it is a good one?

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.530
Well, we try to get there, and then, evaluate
our position.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.490
We may find that we got nowhere with this.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.489
What most people do is abort the program.

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:28.489
Quit the game.

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:29.590
Give up.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:30.780
It’s over.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:34.040
No, don’t despair, it’s not over!

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.750
This is the early stage of teaching an AI,
and this is the time where we can improve

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.010
our predictor, and pick our next objective
more wisely.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:47.880
Over time, you’ll find the ideas that don’t
work, and not only that, you’ll find out

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.610
why they don’t work.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:51.810
Do not worry and aim again.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:56.270
And this leads us to lesson number four: zoom
out and evaluate!

00:03:56.270 --> 00:04:01.069
This is exactly what DeepMind’s amazing
Deep Q-Learning algorithm does that took the

00:04:01.069 --> 00:04:07.680
world by storm as it was able to play Atari
Breakout on a superhuman level just by looking

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.610
at the pixels of the game.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:16.019
This algorithm ran in two phases, where phase
one is collecting experiences, and phase two

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:18.549
was called “experience replay”.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:23.350
This is where the AI stops and reflects upon
these experiences.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:28.800
Zooming out and evaluating is immensely important,
because after all, this is where the real

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.340
learning happens.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:34.210
So every now and then, zoom out, and evaluate.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:39.160
And while we are here, I can simply not resist
adding two more lessons I learned from other

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.780
scientific disciplines.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:46.510
So, lesson number five: if you find something
that works, hold on to it!

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:51.320
This is exactly what Metropolis Light Transport
does, which is a light simulation algorithm

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:56.981
that is able to create beautiful images even
for extremely difficult problems where it

00:04:56.981 --> 00:04:59.740
is challenging to find where the light is.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:05.680
However, when it finally finds something,
it makes sure not to forget about it and explore

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.450
nearby light paths.

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:12.410
It works like a charm for difficult light
transport situations and can create absolutely

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:16.600
beautiful images for even the hardest virtual
scenes.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.110
Seek the light and hold on to it.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:23.970
And whenever you feel that you are still not
making progress, think about the following.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:28.620
Lesson number six: as long as you keep moving,
you’ll keep progressing.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.020
Take a look at this random walk.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:35.240
A random walk is a succession of steps in
completely random directions.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:40.440
This walk is completely lack of direction,
just as a drunkard that tries to find home.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:46.949
However, get this - a mathematical theorem
says that after N steps, the expected distance

00:05:46.949 --> 00:05:51.880
from where we’ve started is proportional
to the square root of N.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:53.280
This is huge!

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.120
What this means, is that for instance, it
we took four completely random steps, we are

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:02.590
expected to be 2 units of distance away from
where we’ve started.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:03.970
That’s progress!

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:09.070
If we take a hundred steps, even then, we
can expect to be around 10 units of distance

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:11.230
away from the starting point.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:16.610
This concept works, even if our predictor
is completely haywire and we choose our objectives

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:17.990
like a drunkard.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:20.470
Now I think that’s a lesson worth sharing.

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.850
To recap: you need an objective!

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:27.060
It can be anything, so long as you keep moving,
you’ll progress.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:32.040
If you have achieved it and it ended up not
being what you were looking for, don’t stop.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.900
Zoom out and reflect.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:38.650
This will help you to improve your predictor
and you will be able to recalibrate and aim

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.229
again at something more meaningful.

00:06:41.229 --> 00:06:44.630
Now, aim and find a new objective!

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:49.750
When you have a new objective, your strategy
needs to change to be able to achieve it.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:55.930
Finally, if you find something desirable,
hold on to it, and explore more in this direction.

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:56.930
Seek the light.

00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:01.840
Of course, you don’t have to live your life
this way, but I think these are interesting,

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.220
mathematically motivated lessons that are
worth showing to you.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:10.050
After all, this series is not only meant to
inform, but to inspire you to get out there

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.229
and create.

00:07:11.229 --> 00:07:16.280
It always feels absolutely amazing getting
these kind messages from you Fellow Scholars,

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.949
some of you said that the series has changed
your life in a positive way.

00:07:19.949 --> 00:07:24.840
I am really out of words and am honored to
be able to make these videos for you Fellow

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:25.940
Scholars.

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:30.320
Let me know in the comments whether you enjoyed
this episode and please, keep the kind messages

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.300
coming, they really make my day.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.490
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

